              "Name": "Leaf",
              "ResourceName": null,
              "Children": [
                {
                  "$type": "Models.Functions.Constant, Models",
                  "FixedValue": 55.0,
                  "Units": null,
                  "Name": "Phyllochron",
                  "ResourceName": null,
                  "Children": [],
                  "Enabled": true,
                  "ReadOnly": false
                },
                {
                  "$type": "Models.Functions.SupplyFunctions.RUEModel, Models",
                  "Name": "Photosynthesis",
                  "ResourceName": null,
                  "Children": [
                    {
                      "$type": "Models.Functions.Constant, Models",
                      "FixedValue": 1.4,
                      "Units": null,
                      "Name": "RUE",
                      "ResourceName": null,
                      "Children": [
                        {
                          "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
                          "Text": "In contrast to Classic, the RUE value in NG refers to the whole plant (above and below ground).\n\nKoocheki et al. (2016) estimated the aboveground RUE of Sesame to be 1.14 g/MJ. This value was adjusted to include root dry mass. Hamedani et al. (2020) estimate the root-to-shoot ratio of six sesame genotypes to be 0.17 - 0.36, averaged over two seasons and under rainfed and irrigated regimes. Therefore, the RUE of 1.14 g/MJ was multiplied by 1.2 (assuming a root-to-shoot ratio of 0.25), resulting in ~1.4 g/MJ.\n\n- Koocheki, A., Nassiri Mahallati, M., Solouki, H., & Karbor, S. (2016). Evaluation of Radiation Absorption and use Efficiency in Substitution Intercropping of Sesame (Sesamum Indicum L.) and Mung Bean (Vigna Radiata L.). Advances in Plants & Agriculture Research, 3(5). https://doi.org/10.15406/apar.2016.03.00109.\n\n- Hamedani, N. G., Gholamhoseini, M., Bazrafshan, F., Amiri, B., & Habibzadeh, F. (2020). Variability of root traits in sesame genotypes under different irrigation regimes. Rhizosphere, 13, 100190. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rhisph.2020.100190.",
                          "Name": "Memo",
                          "ResourceName": null,
                          "Children": [],
                          "Enabled": true,
                          "ReadOnly": false
                        }
                      ],
                      "Enabled": true,
                      "ReadOnly": false
                    },
                    {
                      "$type": "Models.Functions.MultiplyFunction, Models",
                      "Name": "FW",
                      "ResourceName": null,
                      "Children": [
                        {
                          "$type": "Models.Functions.LinearInterpolationFunction, Models",
                          "Name": "LeafFW",
                          "ResourceName": null,
                          "Children": [
                            {
                              "$type": "Models.Functions.XYPairs, Models",
                              "X": [
                                0.0,
                                1.0
                              ],
                              "Y": [
                                0.0,
                                1.0
                              ],
                              "XVariableName": null,
                              "Name": "XYPairs",
                              "ResourceName": null,
                              "Children": [],
                              "Enabled": true,
                              "ReadOnly": false
                            },
                            {
                              "$type": "Models.Functions.VariableReference, Models",
                              "VariableName": "[Leaf].Fw",
                              "Name": "XValue",
                              "ResourceName": null,
                              "Children": [],
